Looking at the problem, I need to find the condition on m and n for a constructible rectangle, then count valid pairs up to 100.

## Setup

Place the rectangle as an m×n grid of unit squares. For square (i,j), denote its four side colors as L(i,j), R(i,j), T(i,j), B(i,j), with all four distinct (a permutation of {R,Y,B,G}).

**Joining condition:** R(i,j) = L(i+1,j) and T(i,j) = B(i,j+1).

**Boundary condition:** All left sides in column 1 share color c_left; all right sides in column m share c_right; all bottom sides in row 1 share c_bottom; all top sides in row n share c_top; with {c_left, c_bottom, c_right, c_top} = {R,Y,B,G}.

## Key Structural Constraint

Define horizontal edges in row j: h₀ = L(1,j), h₁ = R(1,j), ..., hₘ = R(m,j), where hᵢ₋₁ = L(i,j) and hᵢ = R(i,j).

Define vertical edges in column i: v₀ = B(i,1), v₁ = T(i,1), ..., vₙ = T(i,n), where vⱼ₋₁ = B(i,j) and vⱼ = T(i,j).

For each square (i,j): {hᵢ₋₁, hᵢ, vⱼ₋₁, vⱼ} = {R,Y,B,G} with hᵢ₋₁ ≠ hᵢ and vⱼ₋₁ ≠ vⱼ. So {hᵢ₋₁, hᵢ} and {vⱼ₋₁, vⱼ} are **complementary 2-element subsets** of {R,Y,B,G}.

**Fixing j:** {vⱼ₋₁, vⱼ} depends only on j, so {hᵢ₋₁, hᵢ} = complement of {vⱼ₋₁, vⱼ} for all i — meaning {hᵢ₋₁, hᵢ} is the **same set H for all i**.

**Fixing i:** {hᵢ₋₁, hᵢ} = H for all i, so {vⱼ₋₁, vⱼ} = complement of H for all j — meaning {vⱼ₋₁, vⱼ} is the **same set V for all j**.

Thus H and V partition {R,Y,B,G}: {c_left, c_right} = H and {c_bottom, c_top} = V.

## Deriving the Parity Condition

**Horizontal sequence** h₀, h₁, ..., hₘ: consecutive terms alternate between the two elements of H (since {hᵢ₋₁, hᵢ} = H and hᵢ₋₁ ≠ hᵢ). With h₀ = c_left:
- hᵢ = c_left if i even, hᵢ = other element of H if i odd.
- hₘ = c_right requires: if m even → c_right = c_left (contradiction, boundary colors distinct); if **m odd** → c_right = other element of H ✓.

**Vertical sequence** v₀, v₁, ..., vₙ: same alternating argument with V. With v₀ = c_bottom:
- vⱼ = c_bottom if j even, vⱼ = other element of V if j odd.
- vₙ = c_top requires: if n even → c_top = c_bottom (contradiction); if **n odd** → c_top = other element of V ✓.

## Necessity and Sufficiency

**Necessary:** m and n must both be odd (shown above — even m or n forces two boundary colors to coincide).

**Sufficient (construction when m, n both odd):** Choose any partition H ∪ V = {R,Y,B,G}. Set c_left, c_right to the two elements of H; c_bottom, c_top to the two elements of V. Define:
- hᵢ = c_left (i even), c_right (i odd) — since m is odd, hₘ = c_right ✓
- vⱼ = c_bottom (j even), c_top (j odd) — since n is odd, vₙ = c_top ✓

Each square (i,j) gets sides {hᵢ₋₁, hᵢ} = H and {vⱼ₋₁, vⱼ} = V, which are complementary, so all four colors are distinct. Boundary colors are {c_left, c_right, c_bottom, c_top} = {R,Y,B,G}, all distinct. ✓

**Verification with m=2:** The alternating sequence gives h₀ = c_left, h₁ = other, h₂ = c_left = c_right, contradicting distinctness. Confirms even m fails.

## Condition and Count

**The condition is: m and n are both odd.**

Odd integers in [1, 100]: 1, 3, 5, ..., 99 — exactly **50** values.

Number of valid pairs (m, n): 50 × 50 = **2500**.

